1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,072
How do you know exactly what is running inside a bank?

2
00:00:03,122 --> 00:00:08,736
In Lesson 39, the same install ran on a laptop, a test server and production.

3
00:00:08,786 --> 00:00:13,848
Only the values changed, so the product itself must be exactly the same too.

4
00:00:13,898 --> 00:00:18,432
This lesson numbers each release, so everyone knows exactly what is running.

5
00:00:18,482 --> 00:00:21,984
Picture a product that always runs whatever is newest.

6
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:26,712
On Tuesday, a rebuild quietly pulls a newer version nobody tested.

7
00:00:26,762 --> 00:00:32,064
On Wednesday, someone rolls back, pulls the newest again, and gets the same broken build.

8
00:00:32,114 --> 00:00:37,464
Then the bank's assessor asks: what exactly runs in your card zone, and what is inside it?

9
00:00:37,514 --> 00:00:39,336
Nobody can answer.

10
00:00:39,386 --> 00:00:41,760
A moving name can never say what runs.

11
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:43,632
Think of a book.

12
00:00:43,682 --> 00:00:46,800
Its second edition never changes once it is printed.

13
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:52,080
When you quote it, you say which edition you read, and anyone can find the same page.

14
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:54,000
Releases work the same way.

15
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:57,744
Each part of the system numbers its versions in three parts.

16
00:00:57,794 --> 00:01:01,920
The first number grows when a change breaks something callers rely on.

17
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:04,200
The second grows when something is added.

18
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:06,336
The third grows for a fix.

19
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:09,192
So the number alone tells you how careful to be.

20
00:01:09,242 --> 00:01:15,816
A release is one numbered version, made once, from one exact state of the code, and never changed after.

21
00:01:15,866 --> 00:01:19,440
Everything is built from it, and published under its number.

22
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:26,208
Each built piece carries a bill of materials: a list of every component inside it, each with its version.

23
00:01:26,258 --> 00:01:28,656
It also carries a record of how it was built.

24
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:32,976
So the assessor's question has an answer, written on the piece itself.

25
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:35,256
Every install pins its versions.

26
00:01:35,306 --> 00:01:39,528
A pin is one line that names the exact release a product runs.

27
00:01:39,578 --> 00:01:44,616
There is one line for the card zone, one for ecom, and one for the portals.

28
00:01:44,666 --> 00:01:48,408
Every environment runs real releases, the laptop too.

29
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:51,624
So what a builder tests is exactly what the bank runs.

30
00:01:51,674 --> 00:01:53,928
An upgrade is four steps.

31
00:01:53,978 --> 00:01:54,792
Back up.

32
00:01:54,842 --> 00:01:56,664
Change the pinned line.

33
00:01:56,714 --> 00:01:57,480
Deploy.

34
00:01:57,530 --> 00:01:58,272
Check.

35
00:01:58,322 --> 00:02:01,920
The card zone goes first, before ecom, which depends on it.

36
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:07,608
The release notes are read before starting: they say what changed, and whether a new value is needed.

37
00:02:07,658 --> 00:02:10,176
And the database only moves forward.

38
00:02:10,226 --> 00:02:14,280
A release can change the shape of the database, but never steps it back.

39
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:19,656
So rolling back across such a change means restoring the backup taken just before.

40
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:22,080
That is why the backup is always step one.

41
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:23,952
Let's watch the product.

42
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:25,824
An operator plans an upgrade.

43
00:02:25,874 --> 00:02:27,696
They look at the list of releases.

44
00:02:27,746 --> 00:02:29,904
Each has a plain number and its notes.

45
00:02:29,954 --> 00:02:32,904
They choose the newest, and read its notes first.

46
00:02:32,954 --> 00:02:38,280
The notes say whether a breaking change is inside, and whether the installer will ask for a new value.

47
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:41,112
On the server, the operator takes the backup.

48
00:02:41,162 --> 00:02:46,416
Then they change one line: the card zone's pinned release, from the old number to the new one.

49
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:48,288
They deploy.

50
00:02:48,338 --> 00:02:51,648
The services restart on the new release, in order.

51
00:02:51,698 --> 00:02:54,432
The health check runs: every service healthy.

52
00:02:54,482 --> 00:02:57,096
Only then do they do the same for ecom.

53
00:02:57,146 --> 00:03:01,248
Later, the assessor asks again: what is inside the card zone?

54
00:03:01,298 --> 00:03:04,992
The operator opens the bill of materials of one of its pieces.

55
00:03:05,042 --> 00:03:12,072
It lists every component inside, each with its version, and the count sits at the bottom: the answer to the question.

56
00:03:12,122 --> 00:03:13,944
What does this protect?

57
00:03:13,994 --> 00:03:19,488
It protects the bank from surprises: nothing changes unless someone changes a pinned line.

58
00:03:19,538 --> 00:03:23,736
It protects a rollback, because an old release is still exactly what it was.

59
00:03:23,786 --> 00:03:28,656
It protects the assessor's trust, because every piece can say what it contains.

60
00:03:28,706 --> 00:03:33,264
And it protects the data, because the backup always comes before the change.

61
00:03:33,314 --> 00:03:35,136
Back to the bookshelf.

62
00:03:35,186 --> 00:03:41,736
Every edition named by its number, none ever changed after printing, and each with its contents listed.

63
00:03:41,786 --> 00:03:44,232
Next: who is responsible for what.
